// Link the Rust engine into the app binary.
//
// vb-ffi builds as a static library, and this is what pulls it into the
// Runner target. It lives in an xcconfig rather than in the Xcode project so
// the setting is reviewable as text and survives a Flutter template update.
//
// Three flags, each load-bearing, each learned the hard way:
//
//   -force_load    No Swift or Objective-C code references the engine, so
//                  without it the linker takes nothing from the archive and
//                  the app builds clean with no engine in it.
//   -u _vb_*       Release builds dead-strip. Once loaded, the engine's code
//                  is still unreferenced, so it is stripped again unless each
//                  entry point is named as a root. One -u per C ABI function;
//                  add a line here when vb-ffi gains one.
//   -export_dynamic  The Dart side looks the engine up in the running process
//                  (DynamicLibrary.process), which needs these symbols in the
//                  executable's export table, not merely present in it.
//
// -liconv is what `rustc --print native-static-libs` asks for on macOS; the
// rest (libc, libm, libSystem) the app already links.
//
// Always the release engine, whatever configuration the app is built in: a
// debug build of the engine is an order of magnitude slower, and the engine
// is a dependency here, not the thing under development. Build it with
// scripts/build-macos-native.sh, or `cargo build --release -p vb-ffi`.
VB_ENGINE_LIB = $(SRCROOT)/../../core/target/release/libvb_ffi.a
// vb_call reaches every operation added since the first five, so this list
// stops growing with each feature: new operations are names inside vb_call.
VB_ENGINE_ROOTS = -u _vb_version -u _vb_doctor -u _vb_templates -u _vb_browse -u _vb_run -u _vb_call -u _vb_free_string

// What the engine's own native code needs, as `rustc --print
// native-static-libs` reports it for `--features libav,whisper`:
//
//   whisper.cpp   C++ (libc++), and Metal, MetalKit and Accelerate, which it
//                 runs speech recognition on.
//   vb-apple      Vision and Core Image: scene labels, faces, face crops.
//
// A static library carries none of this, so the app names it again; leave
// one out and the link fails with an undefined symbol, which at least is loud.
VB_NATIVE_LIBS = -lc++ -framework Accelerate -framework Foundation -framework Metal -framework MetalKit -framework Vision -framework CoreImage

// The LGPL ffmpeg the engine calls, built by scripts/build-ffmpeg-macos.sh.
// The app carries these libraries inside its own bundle, so it analyses video
// on a Mac that has never installed ffmpeg. `current` is a symlink that
// script maintains, which keeps the ffmpeg version in the script alone.
//
// The engine's own build.rs emits these same flags when cargo links a binary,
// but a static library carries no link dependencies, so the app has to name
// them again here. Dependents first, as static linking requires.
VB_LIBAV_DIR = $(SRCROOT)/../../build/ffmpeg-macos/current
VB_LIBAV_LIBS = -L$(VB_LIBAV_DIR)/lib -lavfilter -lavformat -lavcodec -lswscale -lswresample -lavutil

OTHER_LDFLAGS = $(inherited) -force_load $(VB_ENGINE_LIB) $(VB_ENGINE_ROOTS) $(VB_LIBAV_LIBS) $(VB_NATIVE_LIBS) -Wl,-export_dynamic -liconv

// Apple Silicon only, because the engine is built for the host and cargo has
// no x86_64-apple-darwin target installed here. A universal app needs that
// target added, both slices built, and `lipo -create` over the two archives;
// then this line goes away. Flutter is heading the same way: its
// `--enable-macos-arm64-only` becomes the default in a future release.
ARCHS = arm64
